                },
                "stdout_tail": "{\"actual_models\": [\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\", \"claude-opus-5\"], \"selected_family_models\": [\"claude-opus-5\"], \"other_reported_models\": [\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\"], \"verdict\": {\"summary\": \"The five new boundaries are real and correctly placed. `linux_runtime_checkpoint` is `#[cfg(test)]`-gated at every call site in `runner/linux/runtime.rs`, requires two env vars, and validates the rendezvous root (owner-only dir, uid==geteuid, canonicalized under CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR) before parking \\u2014 no installed fault hook exists. The controller kills the exact owned child (`assert_eq!(checkpoint[\\\"pid\\\"], subject.0.id())`, then `kill()` + non-success `wait()`), so these are genuine SIGKILLs, not injected `Result` errors. The durable expectations are fail-closed and mutually distinguishing: `seal` null except at `sealed`; `release_intent` null only at `ready`; `consumed` None/false/true across the three regimes; `leases state='granted'` stays 1 through kernel cleanup and drops to 0 only after the coordinator's Released reply; the same authenticated reply replays with `accept(..)==false`; `COUNT(*) FROM invocations == 1`; and the stdout marker count is asserted both present (released/sealed) and absent (ready/release-intent/consumed), so the `consumed` vs `released` pair separates commit-without-resume from actual resume while durable state is identical. Recovery keeps the launch uncertain at all five stages (`reconciliation_candidates().len()==1`) and only reaches ProvenEmpty through the retained journal + `Boundary::kill_and_seal`. I re-checked the four prior allegations against the supplied code and they do not reproduce: `record_unused_cleanup` does require `consumed` and writes `cleanup_json`; `CandidateUpsert` reuse is rejected as `stale`; the Release request is drawn from `manager::pending` after `queue::maintain`; an issued Ticket is not treated as consumed. I found no product defect. Two evidence gaps remain, one of which is a single assertion inside the existing loop.\", \"findings\": [\"MEDIUM \\u2014 missing coverage (not a proven defect), scope: src/store/attached/tests/crash.rs, all five stages. The test binds `let Command::Release { release } = &pending.command` and then only uses `release.key` and `release.sealed_sequence`; it never inspects `release.tickets`, and the journal seal is printed (`\\\"seal\\\": after[..][\\\"seal\\\"]`) but only asserted for stability at the `sealed` stage. So the one bit that makes a recovered Grant a *potentially used* Grant is unverified after every real crash. This matters most at `release-intent`, where the two durable sources deliberately disagree: SQLite says `consumed == Some(false)` (asserted by the test) while `Journal::cleanup` sets `possibly_released = record.release_intent.is_some()` \\u2014 true \\u2014 per its own contract comment (\\\"A crash after this point is possibly-released even if the kernel barrier was never actually resumed\\\"). `Registry::persist_cleanup` forwards only `seal.boundary_sha256` and `seal.sha256()` and passes `never: None` to `record_attached_cleanup`, so nothing in the supplied composition demonstrates which source wins in the recovered Release. Existing coverage does not close this: the kernel happy-path fixture asserts `cleanup_json.user_code_released == true` for a live successful run reconstructed from in-memory `progress`, and the `release.rs` unit test asserts `user_code_released == false` for the in-process NeverReleased proof; neither reconstructs the flag from durable state alone after a process death. Smallest material addition (one assertion, inside the existing stage loop, no new stages): after obtaining `pending`, assert that the ticket cleanup carried in `release.tickets` reports potential use exactly as the durable seal does \\u2014 i.e. compare it against `after[\\\"state\\\"][\\\"records\\\"][&key][\\\"seal\\\"][\\\"possibly_released\\\"]` rather than hardcoding a polarity \\u2014 and assert `release.tickets` is empty at `ready`, where no Ticket was ever issued. Formulating it as an equality keeps the acceptance design-agnostic while proving the potentially-used bit survives crash recovery.\", \"LOW-MEDIUM \\u2014 contract clause not evidenced in the supplied composition; scope qualifier: this is an absence-of-evidence finding over the material provided, not an inference about unshown coordinator code, and it is satisfied if a TTL/deadline test exists outside the listed set. M-A04 names two detections after local commit \\u2014 second user launch, and TTL-release of a potentially used Grant. The first is adequately composed: durable `consume_ticket` idempotence across a reopened manager database (tests/support/machine_manager.rs), barrier-level `Disposition::AlreadyConsumed` / non-replayed consumption (release.rs unit tests), plus the new test's `COUNT(*) FROM invocations == 1` and stdout marker counts proving recovery itself started nothing. The second has no counterpart anywhere in the supplied evidence: `GrantSnapshot.offer_deadline_unix_millis` exists and the manager fixture only polls for `GrantState::Offered`, and no test lets an offer/lease deadline elapse while a Ticket is outstanding. The nearest analogue is a different trigger \\u2014 the `reset_after_ticket` path (coordinator store wiped, authority rotated, `retired_sequence_floor`, blocker held until `record_cleanup` + `seal_release`) \\u2014 which proves rotation-driven, not expiry-driven, retention. This belongs on the native-coordinator side, not in this fixture, whose coordinator replies are modeled. Smallest material addition: one native-coordinator acceptance that a Grant whose TTL/offer deadline elapses while a Ticket has been issued is not reclaimed, re-offered, or re-armed until sealed cleanup evidence is recorded \\u2014 no crash-stage permutation matrix needed.\"], \"verdict\": \"findings\"}}\n",
                "stderr_tail": ""
              }
            ]
          }
